Read enough of the repository to tell the difference — the request alone is
often ambiguous, and the code usually settles it.

## The kinds

**bug-fix** — something already built behaves wrongly. There is a defect to
reproduce: a stack trace, a wrong value, a monitor that fired, a reported
symptom. The change is expected to be small and local, and what "correct" means
is not in question.

**cr-fix** — addressing findings a reviewer already raised on existing work.
The analysis has been done by someone else; this is carrying it out.

**feature-dev** — new capability, changed behaviour that someone has to decide
on, or a change large enough that how to build it is a real question. Anything
touching a contract other code depends on, a data model, or several modules at
once belongs here even when the request sounds small.

## What the choice costs

A **bug-fix** or **cr-fix** takes the short path: intent, spec, then straight to
building. No design, no design critique, no plan. That is right when the work
is understood and small, and wrong when it is not — a defect whose cause is
unknown, or whose fix touches a contract, is feature-dev however it was
reported.

**feature-dev** takes the full path, which costs four human gates and several
more turns. Choose it when the work genuinely needs deciding, not to be safe.

If you are unsure, say so in the document and choose **feature-dev**: an
unnecessary design is wasted effort, but an unplanned change to something that
needed planning is a defect nobody scoped.

## The document

State the kind, what in the request and the code led you there, what you
expect the change to touch, and anything that would change your mind. Be
specific: "the NPE at RedisShardedTaskQueue:162 has a clear cause and a local
fix" is useful; "this looks like a bug" is not.

## The repositories this touches

The task names one repository, because someone had to type something. Say
whether the change actually stays there. A contract and its callers, a shared
service and the consumer that must move with it, a Jira issue that spans
services by design -- all of those need more than one, and naming them here is
what gets them checked out for every turn that follows.

Name them as applications, the way people do (`sample_service`), or as
clone URLs where an application record does not exist. Do not list the task's
own repository; it is already here. Do not list a repository you merely read
for context -- only ones the change will touch.

End with two lines, exactly one `KIND` and one `REPOS`:

```
KIND: bug-fix
KIND: cr-fix
KIND: feature-dev

REPOS: none
REPOS: sample_service, sample-web
```

